<article class="env-corollary" id="2DB076">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/2DB076">Corollary <span data-tag="2DB076">2.3.4.8</span>.</a>$n$-categories!and functor categories Let $\calC $ be an $n$-category and $X$ a simplicial set. Then $\Fun (X,\calC )$ is an $n$-category. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Proposition <a href="/tag/9F5D0C" data-tag="9F5D0C">1.2.7.3</a> asserts that $\Fun (X,\calC )$ is an $\infty $-category. We will show that $\Fun (X,\calC )$ satisfies condition $(2)$ of Proposition <a href="/tag/4814A2" data-tag="4814A2">2.3.4.7</a>. Suppose given a pair of maps $f,f': K \rightarrow \Fun (X,\calC )$ such that $f| \sk ^{n} K$ and $f'| \sk ^{n} K$ are homotopic relative to $f| \sk ^{n-1} K$. We wish to show that $f = f'$. We may identify $f$ and $f'$ with maps $F,F': K \times X \rightarrow \calC $. Since $\calC $ is an $n$-category, to prove that $F = F'$ it suffices to show that $F | \sk ^{n}(K \times X)$ and $F' | \sk ^{n}(K \times X)$ are homotopic relative to $\sk ^{n-1}(K \times X)$. This follows at once, since $\sk ^{p}(K \times X) \subseteq (\sk ^{p} K) \times X$ for every integer $p$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>